Specificity / Evidentiary Overstatement: The narrative conclusively labels the customer’s business a shell company based primarily on recent formation, use of a virtual office, limited public footprint, and unusual transaction activity. Those facts may support heightened concern or possible shell-company use, but they do not establish that the company lacks legitimate operations or exists solely to move illicit funds.


Continental Commercial Bank is filing this SAR on customer Meridian Technical Solutions LLC and its sole owner, Brian Keller, age 43, of Scottsdale, Arizona, regarding suspected pass-through activity and possible misuse of a recently established business account.

Meridian Technical Solutions LLC opened business checking account 90641782 on March 12, 2026. At onboarding, Keller identified Meridian as an information technology consulting company providing network installation, cloud migration, and technical support services to small and midsized businesses. Keller stated that he was the company’s sole owner and manager and expected monthly account activity of approximately $40,000 to $70,000.

State business records show Meridian Technical Solutions LLC was formed in Arizona on February 24, 2026 and remains in active status. Keller is listed as the company’s manager and beneficial owner. The company uses a commercial virtual-office address in Phoenix, Arizona. Keller provided an Internal Revenue Service employer identification number, articles of organization, and a business-services agreement associated with the listed address during onboarding.

From March 12 through May 31, 2026, account activity was limited. Meridian received approximately $38,500 in ACH and check payments from four businesses and made payments for software subscriptions, telecommunications services, accounting services, office supplies, and transfers to Keller’s personal account.

Beginning June 4, 2026, the volume and nature of activity increased substantially.

Between June 4 and August 20, 2026, Meridian received 17 incoming domestic wires and ACH credits totaling $684,300 from 12 commercial counterparties located in Arizona, California, Nevada, Texas, Colorado, and Washington.

Individual incoming payments ranged from $18,500 to $62,000. Payment descriptions included “IT services,” “implementation,” “network project,” “consulting,” and “invoice payment.”

Within one to three business days of receiving 14 of the 17 payments, Meridian sent outgoing wires totaling $592,700 to five technology distributors and consulting companies.

The largest outgoing counterparties were:

Westlake Systems Distribution Inc., $188,500 across four wires.
Horizon Network Supply LLC, $146,200 across three wires.
Northstar Technology Partners Inc., $104,000 across two wires.
Pacific Data Consulting LLC, $91,500 across three wires.
Apex Infrastructure Services LLC, $62,500 across two wires.

Public business records identified all five outgoing counterparties as active U.S. business entities. Continental identified no common ownership between Keller, Meridian, and the five companies through available corporate records.

Meridian retained approximately $91,600 of the incoming funds during the review period. The retained funds were used for payments including software vendors, telecommunications expenses, accounting services, credit card payments, transfers to Keller, and account balances remaining at Continental.

Historical review was limited because Meridian had been a Continental customer for less than six months.

On July 9, 2026, Continental contacted Keller regarding the increased volume of incoming and outgoing payments. Keller stated that Meridian had recently obtained several subcontracting engagements supporting larger technology implementation projects.

Keller explained that customers paid Meridian for project work and that Meridian then purchased hardware, software, and specialized subcontractor services from other companies. He stated that this structure accounted for the rapid movement of a large portion of incoming payments to technology-related counterparties.

Keller provided four customer invoices totaling $176,000 and three vendor invoices totaling $139,500.

The customer invoices described network migration, infrastructure support, and systems-integration work. The vendor invoices described network equipment, software licensing, and technical consulting services. The amounts generally corresponded to activity observed in Meridian’s account.

Continental was able to verify that two of the companies shown on the customer invoices maintained active websites and public business registrations. One customer’s website listed Meridian Technical Solutions LLC as a subcontractor on a technology implementation project completed in July 2026.

Continental independently identified public websites for three of Meridian’s five primary outgoing counterparties. Two described themselves as technology distributors, while one advertised network engineering and consulting services.

Continental could not identify a dedicated public website for Meridian Technical Solutions LLC. Online searches produced the company’s state registration, the virtual-office address, several business-directory entries, and the subcontractor reference described above.

Continental did not identify a public employee roster or professional networking profiles clearly associated with Meridian other than Keller’s personal professional profile. Keller’s profile described prior employment in information technology project management and systems administration.

Continental did not identify a separate physical office occupied by Meridian. The company’s listed address is a virtual-office facility used by multiple unrelated businesses.

On August 3, 2026, Continental requested additional documentation concerning Meridian’s business activity. Keller provided two project statements of work, additional customer correspondence, and purchase documentation related to network equipment.

One statement of work identified Meridian as a subcontractor responsible for network configuration and deployment support on a commercial office project. The second described cloud-migration support for a regional professional-services company.

Continental did not independently verify completion of all services reflected in the documentation and could not determine whether every incoming payment during the review period corresponded to a completed project.

Continental identified no significant cash activity, international wire activity, cryptocurrency transactions, sanctions matches, or payments to jurisdictions identified by the bank as high risk during the review period.

Continental considers the activity suspicious because Meridian experienced a substantial increase in transaction volume shortly after account opening and rapidly transferred a large portion of incoming customer payments to other businesses. The company is recently formed, operates from a virtual-office address, and has a limited independent public footprint.

The rapid movement of funds and limited operating history are consistent with possible pass-through activity and warrant additional review of Meridian’s stated business purpose and underlying commercial relationships.

Continental determined that Meridian Technical Solutions LLC is a shell company created by Keller to receive and redistribute funds while concealing the true parties conducting the underlying transactions. The company’s recent formation, virtual-office address, absence of a dedicated website, limited identified employee presence, and rapid movement of incoming payments establish that Meridian does not conduct legitimate business operations.

Continental placed Meridian’s account under enhanced monitoring on August 21, 2026. The account remains open. Continental maintains account statements, wire and ACH records, customer invoices, vendor invoices, project statements of work, customer communications, onboarding records, public-record research, and customer due diligence information and will make those records available to appropriate authorities upon request.
